      "Announcement emails learned their one allowed image: the official 'Get it on Google Play' badge. The email dialect stays deliberately tiny — bold, italics, links, and now a badge tag that drops in the store artwork already linked to the listing; no other image can ever ride into an inbox from a template. The launch announcement to existing users goes out wearing it.",
      "The Android app stops feeling frozen at launch (operator: 'people think the app crashed'). The splash screen used to wait for the whole app engine to download and start before dismissing — seconds of motionless icon on a phone connection; it now dismisses on the first painted frame. And what it dismisses onto was rebuilt from a filmed reference of how the big streaming apps open: a full-screen brand hold — the Picacho wordmark with three quietly pulsing dots beneath it — that moves from its very first frame and dissolves the instant the app is actually ready, replacing the old fixed-length logo animation that gave no sign anything was happening. Motion is the whole trick: a wordmark with pulsing dots reads as working, a motionless icon reads as crashed, no matter how long either stays. The icon phase itself got shorter too: the app used to hold its first byte until every database query behind the page had finished — 0.7 to 1.5 measured seconds of extra frozen icon — and now the brand hold streams to the phone immediately while those queries run behind it. Bonus fix found while reproducing: with no internet at all, the splash used to stay up forever in front of the 'Can't reach Picacho' page — the offline screen now dismisses it too. An app update (1.8.3 on Google Play) completes the picture natively: the launch screen itself is now the Picacho wordmark on a clean field instead of an icon in a circle — the same frame the loading hold opens with, so from tap to app it reads as one continuous surface — it can never hang more than a few seconds even with no connection, and after a happy rating in the feedback card the app can now ask for a Play Store review without leaving the app.",
      "The Android launch screen finally changes, and the reason the last attempt did nothing is worth recording: Android draws its own splash over everything at startup, and unless an app explicitly names an icon for it, the system falls back to the plain launcher icon — so the branded screen shipped in the previous update was being drawn underneath and was never visible on any phone. The launch now shows the Picacho mark with its ochre rule sweeping beneath it as a live progress cue, in light and dark variants, moving for as long as the app takes to open. Motion is the entire point: a still frame reads as a crash however brief it is, a moving one reads as working however long it takes.",
      "The home-screen icon got the layer it was missing. Since Android 13, phones can re-tint every app icon to match your wallpaper, and they do it from a dedicated silhouette the app is supposed to provide — Picacho never provided one, which left each phone's launcher to improvise from the full-colour icon instead, and different launchers (and in some cases the same launcher in light versus dark) improvise differently. That is the most likely source of the icon appearing larger or shifted in one theme. The silhouette now ships, drawn from the same artwork at the same position, so the mark can no longer move or resize between themes.",
      "Opening the app got measurably shorter on the server side too, and this half reaches phones without any update. Two things used to happen inside that frozen launch: every signed-in request made a full round trip to the authentication server on the other side of the Atlantic (the app now verifies the session's signature locally instead, while still refreshing it exactly as before), and opening the app while signed out downloaded the entire app and started it up just to discover it should show the login screen (that is now an immediate redirect — 6 bytes instead of 20,000 plus the whole bundle).",
      "Scrolling in the app stops needing two swipes (operator: 'you swipe, it moves and stops, you have to reswipe'). Root cause, measured on an instrumented emulator: every app page secretly had TWO scrollable layers — the real content pane, and a parasitic 76-pixel scroll on the page itself created by the screen-notch padding — and Android never carries a swipe's momentum across that boundary, so a flick that landed on the wrong layer moved a few dozen pixels and stopped dead. The page layer is now pinned shut; one scroller owns every swipe, verified live: the same flick that died at the boundary now sails 4,500 pixels through the templates grid.",
